TI  - Analysis of HIV and HBV co-dynamics in vivo
PY  - 2025
T2  - Journal of Mathematics and Computer Science
VL  - 40
IS  - 2
SP  - 240
EP  - 272
DO  - 10.22436/jmcs.040.02.07
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105011506685&doi=10.22436%2fjmcs.040.02.07&partnerID=40&md5=ce618f6306315ca8b8300658b33a139b
AB  - Co-infections involving human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) and hepatitis B virus (HBV) are relatively frequent due to their shared transmission pathways. Mathematical models are essential for investigating the dynamics of multiple viral infections within a host. The formulation of mathematical models for within-host HIV and HBV co-infection has received limited attention. This study introduces a novel model to investigate the dynamics of HIV and HBV co-infection within a host. The model is built on the understanding that HBV infects hepatocytes, while HIV targets both CD4+ T cells and hepatocytes. The framework is constructed with seven nonlinear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) to describe the interactions between uninfected hepatocytes, hepatocytes infected by HIV, hepatocytes infected by HBV, uninfected CD4+ T cells, CD4+ T cells infected with HIV, and the free viral particles of both HIV and HBV. Initially, the model’s validity is confirmed by proving that its solutions remain non-negative and bounded. The equilibrium states and critical threshold parameters are identified, along with the conditions necessary for their existence and asymptotic stability. The stability of each equilibrium is examined using suitable Lyapunov functions in conjunction with LaSalle’s invariance principle. The theoretical findings are demonstrated through numerical simulations. A sensitivity analysis of the basic reproductive numbers for HIV (R0) and HBV (R1) single infections is performed to identify the key parameters influencing the dynamics of HIV and HBV co-infection. The effect of anti-HIV and anti-HBV drugs has been addressed. We determine the threshold efficacies for anti-HIV and anti-HBV therapies. It is ensured that if treatment efficacies surpass these thresholds, both HIV and HBV may be entirely eradicated. The model we propose, along with its analysis, offers valuable insights into the behavior of co-infections, particularly in cases where viruses infect different types of host cells. Additionally, it may contribute to the design of more effective strategies for the treatment and management of complex viral infections. © 2026, International Scientific Research Publications. All rights reserved..
