AB  - Over the last few decades, within-host mathematical models have been developed with the aim of gaining insight into disease progression in a single infected individual and the potential role of various immune interventions. In this chapter, we describe the general principles of modeling within-host virus dynamics, discuss the usefulness of such models in predicting disease establishment inside an infected individual, explain how models can be used to quantify virus and host characteristics that capture the kinetics apparent in empirical viral data, and explain how empirical data can be used to select among models with various levels of biological complexity. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2021.
