TI  - In vivo kinetics of SARS-CoV-2 infection and its relationship with a person’s infectiousness
PY  - 2021
T2  - Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America
VL  - 118
IS  - 49
C7  - e2111477118
DO  - 10.1073/pnas.2111477118
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85120883476&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.2111477118&partnerID=40&md5=4b933b4299751893503d09f6deefc386
AB  - The within-host viral kinetics of SARS-CoV-2 infection and how they relate to a person’s infectiousness are not well understood. This limits our ability to quantify the impact of interventions on viral transmission. Here, we develop viral dynamic models of SARS-CoV-2 infection and fit them to data to estimate key within-host parameters such as the infected cell half-life and the within-host reproductive number. We then develop a model linking viral load (VL) to infectiousness and show a person’s infectiousness increases sublinearly with VL and that the logarithm of the VL in the upper respiratory tract is a better surrogate of infectiousness than the VL itself. Using data on VL and the predicted infectiousness, we further incorporated data on antigen and RT-PCR tests and compared their usefulness in detecting infection and preventing transmission. We found that RT-PCR tests perform better than antigen tests assuming equal testing frequency; however, more frequent antigen testing may perform equally well with RT-PCR tests at a lower cost but with many more false-negative tests. Overall, our models provide a quantitative framework for inferring the impact of therapeutics and vaccines that lower VL on the infectiousness of individuals and for evaluating rapid testing strategies. © 2021 National Academy of Sciences. All rights reserved.
