TI  - A multi-strain virus model with infected cell age structure: Application to HIV
PY  - 2015
T2  - Nonlinear Analysis: Real World Applications
VL  - 22
SP  - 354
EP  - 372
DO  - 10.1016/j.nonrwa.2014.10.004
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84908374930&doi=10.1016%2fj.nonrwa.2014.10.004&partnerID=40&md5=58e4e461d893dc8f91e08e13d4730067
AB  - A general mathematical model of a within-host viral infection with n virus strains and explicit age-since-infection structure for infected cells is considered. In the model, multiple virus strains compete for a population of target cells. Cells infected with virus strain iε{1,⋯,n} die at per-capita rate δi(a) and produce virions at per-capita rate pi(a), where δi(a) and pi(a) are functions of the age-since-infection of the cell. Viral strain i has a basic reproduction number, Ri, and a corresponding positive single strain equilibrium, Ei, when Ri>1. If Ri<1, then the total concentration of virus strain i will converge to 0 asymptotically. The main result is that when maxiRi>1 and all of the reproduction numbers are distinct, i.e. Ri≈ Rj≈i≈j, the viral strain with the maximal basic reproduction number competitively excludes the other strains. As an application of the model, HIV evolution is considered and simulations are provided. © 2014 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
