TI  - Directly transmitted viral diseases: modeling the dynamics of transmission
PY  - 2008
T2  - Trends in Microbiology
VL  - 16
IS  - 4
SP  - 165
EP  - 172
DO  - 10.1016/j.tim.2008.01.007
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-41549090794&doi=10.1016%2fj.tim.2008.01.007&partnerID=40&md5=f21a58705110dddf99234242c50f615d
AB  - A key hurdle in understanding the spread and control of infectious diseases is to capture appropriately the dynamics of pathogen transmission. As people and goods travel increasingly rapidly around the world, so do pathogens; we must be prepared to understand their spread, in terms of the contact network between hosts, viral life history and within-host dynamics. This will require collaborative work that takes into account viral life history, strategy and evolution, and host genetics, demographics and immunodynamics. Mathematical models are a useful tool for integrating the data and analyses from diverse fields that contribute to our understanding of viral transmission dynamics in heterogeneous host populations. © 2008 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
